00:07:25,000 --> 00:07:27,000
Scoutmastership in seven minutes

185
00:07:27,000 --> 00:07:29,000
or less.

186
00:07:29,000 --> 00:07:31,000
So

187
00:07:31,000 --> 00:07:33,000
the final step in advancement

188
00:07:33,000 --> 00:07:35,000
is recognizing a scout

189
00:07:35,000 --> 00:07:37,000
by presenting them

190
00:07:37,000 --> 00:07:39,000
with the achievements that they've earned,

191
00:07:39,000 --> 00:07:41,000
and this is done

192
00:07:41,000 --> 00:07:43,000
at a ceremony called

193
00:07:43,000 --> 00:07:45,000
Quartz of Honor.

194
00:07:45,000 --> 00:07:47,000
In the Guide to Advancement 2013,

195
00:07:47,000 --> 00:07:49,000
it says this: when the Board of Review

196
00:07:49,000 --> 00:07:51,000
has approved his advancement,

197
00:07:51,000 --> 00:07:53,000
the scout deserves recognition

198
00:07:53,000 --> 00:07:55,000
as soon as possible. This should be done

199
00:07:55,000 --> 00:07:57,000
at a ceremony at the next

200
00:07:57,000 --> 00:07:59,000
unit meeting. The certificate for his

201
00:07:59,000 --> 00:08:01,000
new rank may be presented later

202
00:08:01,000 --> 00:08:03,000
during a formal Quartz of Honor.

203
00:08:03,000 --> 00:08:05,000
While we know that we're headed

204
00:08:05,000 --> 00:08:07,000
towards a Quartz of Honor for a formal

205
00:08:07,000 --> 00:08:09,000
recognition of the scout's achievement,

206
00:08:09,000 --> 00:08:11,000
we want to get him the badge

207
00:08:11,000 --> 00:08:13,000
as soon as possible.

208
00:08:13,000 --> 00:08:15,000
Not only people will do that at the next

209
00:08:15,000 --> 00:08:17,000
troop meeting or crew meeting or whatever,

210
00:08:17,000 --> 00:08:19,000
and just with a

211
00:08:19,000 --> 00:08:21,000
quick recognition,

212
00:08:21,000 --> 00:08:23,000
maybe as a part of the announcements and things like that,

213
00:08:23,000 --> 00:08:25,000
just to make sure that the scout

214
00:08:25,000 --> 00:08:27,000
gets the badge right away.

215
00:08:27,000 --> 00:08:29,000
And then the certificate, the little card

216
00:08:29,000 --> 00:08:31,000
that comes with the badge- you can hold on

217
00:08:31,000 --> 00:08:33,000
to that and give it to

218
00:08:33,000 --> 00:08:35,000
them at the Quartz of Honor.

219
00:08:35,000 --> 00:08:37,000
Now some people worry that if they don't

220
00:08:37,000 --> 00:08:39,000
hold on to the badges the scouts aren't

221
00:08:39,000 --> 00:08:41,000
going to attend the next Quartz of Honor, and I

222
00:08:41,000 --> 00:08:43,000
I guess that's a reasonable concern.

223
00:08:43,000 --> 00:08:45,000
but I'd be more concerned that there may be

224
00:08:45,000 --> 00:08:47,000
a couple of things wrong with the way that

225
00:08:47,000 --> 00:08:49,000
we're presenting Quartz of Honor

226
00:08:49,000 --> 00:08:51,000
that could be causing scouts to stay

227
00:08:51,000 --> 00:08:53,000
away from it, if that's a problem.

228
00:08:53,000 --> 00:08:55,000
So what I want to do is give you

229
00:08:55,000 --> 00:08:57,000
kind of several rules of thumb here,

230
00:08:57,000 --> 00:08:59,000
Just running the Quartz of Honor Now, to my way of thinking,

231
00:08:59,000 --> 00:09:01,000
it should be the senior patrol leader

232
00:09:01,000 --> 00:09:03,000
or scouts in leadership

233
00:09:03,000 --> 00:09:05,000
positions should be running that

234
00:09:05,000 --> 00:09:07,000
Quartz of Honor, not the adults.

235
00:09:07,000 --> 00:09:09,000
At our last troop Quartz of Honor,

236
00:09:09,000 --> 00:09:11,000
one of our senior scouts was working on

237
00:09:11,000 --> 00:09:13,000
communications merit badge and he asked

238
00:09:13,000 --> 00:09:15,000
if he could emcee, and the

239
00:09:15,000 --> 00:09:17,000
senior patrol leader thought that that was a great

240
00:09:17,000 --> 00:09:19,000
idea. so he emceed

241
00:09:19,000 --> 00:09:21,000
the Quartz of Honor and I think

242
00:09:21,000 --> 00:09:23,000
that's a great way to

243
00:09:23,000 --> 00:09:25,000
make things happen.

244
00:09:25,000 --> 00:09:27,000
If you're putting on a Quartz of Honor and the scouts are

245
00:09:27,000 --> 00:09:29,000
kind of meh about it,

246
00:09:29,000 --> 00:09:31,000
it might be that if it's the

247
00:09:31,000 --> 00:09:33,000
adults or the troop committee handing out

248
00:09:33,000 --> 00:09:35,000
badges and the scouts are just kind of watching,

249
00:09:35,000 --> 00:09:37,000
they're not going to be all that interested

250
00:09:37,000 --> 00:09:39,000
in attending right. Another rule of thumb

251
00:09:39,000 --> 00:09:41,000
that will help liven up a Quartz of Honor

252
00:09:41,000 --> 00:09:43,000
is the length of time

253
00:09:43,000 --> 00:09:45,000
that it takes. How long is

254
00:09:45,000 --> 00:09:47,000
a Quartz of Honor supposed to be?

255
00:09:47,000 --> 00:09:49,000
Well, our troop has

256
00:09:49,000 --> 00:09:51,000
35 to 40 scouts on average

257
00:09:51,000 --> 00:09:53,000
and we have a table full of

258
00:09:53,000 --> 00:09:55,000
things to present at most Quartz of Honor,

259
00:09:55,000 --> 00:09:57,000
and my rule of thumb is

260
00:09:57,000 --> 00:09:59,000
that we need that Quartz of Honor to last

261
00:09:59,000 --> 00:10:01,000
less than an hour. 30 to 45

262
00:10:01,000 --> 00:10:03,000
minutes is about where you want

263
00:10:03,000 --> 00:10:05,000
to be. If it's less than that,

264
00:10:05,000 --> 00:10:07,000
it's less than that and that's not a problem.

265
00:10:07,000 --> 00:10:09,000
If we have everybody there for two hours

266
00:10:09,000 --> 00:10:11,000
of long,

267
00:10:11,000 --> 00:10:13,000
formal, drawn out presentations,

268
00:10:13,000 --> 00:10:15,000
you know that's an awful

269
00:10:15,000 --> 00:10:17,000
lot to sit through. If we're presenting

270
00:10:17,000 --> 00:10:19,000
an Eagle Rank, which we do as a part

271
00:10:19,000 --> 00:10:21,000
of our regular Quartz of Honor,

272
00:10:21,000 --> 00:10:23,000
it may go just a bit longer,

273
00:10:23,000 --> 00:10:25,000
but brevity is good. and if we're going to ask families

274
00:10:25,000 --> 00:10:27,000
to be there with younger siblings and things

275
00:10:27,000 --> 00:10:29,000
like that. we want to keep that program

276
00:10:29,000 --> 00:10:31,000
moving along. We don't want to skip

277
00:10:31,000 --> 00:10:33,000
anything. We don't want to artificially

278
00:10:33,000 --> 00:10:35,000
shorten it so that we miss

279
00:10:35,000 --> 00:10:37,000
meaningful moments. Pay attention

280
00:10:37,000 --> 00:10:39,000
to how long that Quartz of Honor is taking.

281
00:10:39,000 --> 00:10:41,000
Next, I would suggest that the program

282
00:10:41,000 --> 00:10:43,000
be formal without

283
00:10:43,000 --> 00:10:45,000
being real, theatrically

284
00:10:45,000 --> 00:10:47,000
overblown. You know, simple words spoken

285
00:10:47,000 --> 00:10:49,000
in honesty are almost always

286
00:10:49,000 --> 00:10:51,000
better, at least to my mind, than a lot of

287
00:10:51,000 --> 00:10:53,000
pageantry. Scouts want

288
00:10:53,000 --> 00:10:55,000
some formality and ceremony,

289
00:10:55,000 --> 00:10:57,000
but not at a point where it's so overblown

290
00:10:57,000 --> 00:10:59,000
that it makes them feel

291
00:10:59,000 --> 00:11:01,000
silly or reluctant to participate

292
00:11:01,000 --> 00:11:03,000
in it. So if the program is formal

293
00:11:03,000 --> 00:11:05,000
and meaningful and well done

294
00:11:05,000 --> 00:11:07,000
and it's not way overblown

295
00:11:07,000 --> 00:11:09,000
and it doesn't have all kinds of

296
00:11:09,000 --> 00:11:11,000
crazy elements thrown into it, Scouts

297
00:11:11,000 --> 00:11:13,000
will enjoy it or

298
00:11:13,000 --> 00:11:15,000
at least endure it. right,

299
00:11:15,000 --> 00:11:17,000
What else is happening

300
00:11:17,000 --> 00:11:19,000
at the Quartz of Honor? I know that

301
00:11:19,000 --> 00:11:21,000
most people usually combine the Quartz of Honor

302
00:11:21,000 --> 00:11:23,000
with some announcements

303
00:11:23,000 --> 00:11:25,000
or discussion with families.

304
00:11:25,000 --> 00:11:27,000
Now we have a parent's business

305
00:11:27,000 --> 00:11:29,000
meeting for a few minutes before

306
00:11:29,000 --> 00:11:31,000
each Quartz of Honor And we have

307
00:11:31,000 --> 00:11:33,000
a Quartz of Honor on a quarterly basis.

308
00:11:33,000 --> 00:11:35,000
We talk to parents about the

309
00:11:35,000 --> 00:11:37,000
scheduling of camping trips

310
00:11:37,000 --> 00:11:39,000
or rechartering or

311
00:11:39,000 --> 00:11:41,000
any kind of business that needs to be taken

312
00:11:41,000 --> 00:11:43,000
care of. That part of the program

313
00:11:43,000 --> 00:11:45,000
needs to be quick. It doesn't

314
00:11:45,000 --> 00:11:47,000
need to be a long, drawn out question

315
00:11:47,000 --> 00:11:49,000
and answer period or something like that,

316
00:11:49,000 --> 00:11:51,000
because everybody's going to be there

317
00:11:51,000 --> 00:11:53,000
once the program is concluded

318
00:11:53,000 --> 00:11:55,000
and we can talk about

319
00:11:55,000 --> 00:11:57,000
details and get into things with

320
00:11:57,000 --> 00:11:59,000
individuals after we've presented

321
00:11:59,000 --> 00:12:01,000
the program. Another rule of thumb is

322
00:12:01,000 --> 00:12:03,000
when you schedule a Quartz of Honor.

323
00:12:03,000 --> 00:12:05,000
Now we schedule our Quartz of Honor

324
00:12:05,000 --> 00:12:07,000
in place of a regular

325
00:12:07,000 --> 00:12:09,000
troop meeting. That way

326
00:12:09,000 --> 00:12:11,000
it's on everyone's schedule already.

327
00:12:11,000 --> 00:12:13,000
They already know that

328
00:12:13,000 --> 00:12:15,000
in our case, Monday nights is scout night,

329
00:12:15,000 --> 00:12:17,000
so they don't have to

330
00:12:17,000 --> 00:12:19,000
add another night to the calendar,

331
00:12:19,000 --> 00:12:21,000
and a family with a couple of active children

332
00:12:21,000 --> 00:12:23,000
is likely to have a pretty

333
00:12:23,000 --> 00:12:25,000
involved schedule of meetings

334
00:12:25,000 --> 00:12:27,000
and activities. I think it's important

335
00:12:27,000 --> 00:12:29,000
to make things easy for families

336
00:12:29,000 --> 00:12:31,000
to remember and easy

337
00:12:31,000 --> 00:12:33,000
for them to fit into their busy

338
00:12:33,000 --> 00:12:35,000
schedules. Now, if you apply

339
00:12:35,000 --> 00:12:37,000
some of these ideas, some of these rules of thumb,

340
00:12:37,000 --> 00:12:39,000
you may find that parents and scouts

341
00:12:39,000 --> 00:12:41,000
are more interested in Quartz of Honor

342
00:12:41,000 --> 00:12:43,000
and they make a point out of attending.

343
00:12:43,000 --> 00:12:45,000
There's little chance you're going to get every single scout

344
00:12:45,000 --> 00:12:47,000
and every single family

345
00:12:47,000 --> 00:12:49,000
to any given Quartz of Honor. so do what I do:

346
00:12:49,000 --> 00:12:51,000
Concentrate on the ones that show up

347
00:12:51,000 --> 00:12:53,000
and change things around

348
00:12:53,000 --> 00:12:55,000
so that more will show up if that's what's needed.

349
00:12:55,000 --> 00:12:57,000
Now, if you're putting on a Quartz of Honor

350
00:12:57,000 --> 00:12:59,000
and nobody's showing up

351
00:12:59,000 --> 00:13:01,000
or you feel that the attendance is way down,

352
00:13:01,000 --> 00:13:03,000
the first thing we ought to do

353
00:13:03,000 --> 00:13:05,000
is look at what we're doing

354
00:13:05,000 --> 00:13:07,000
and why we're doing it

355
00:13:07,000 --> 00:13:09,000
and understand that scouts and families

356
00:13:09,000 --> 00:13:11,000
vote with their feet on things like this.

357
00:13:11,000 --> 00:13:13,000
If they aren't showing up,

358
00:13:13,000 --> 00:13:15,000
it's likely that we have something off base

359
00:13:15,000 --> 00:13:17,000
and we need to make adjustments.

360
00:13:17,000 --> 00:13:19,000
So just to run over that advice one more time:

361
00:13:19,000 --> 00:13:21,000
remember that right after a border review

362
00:13:21,000 --> 00:13:23,000
and a scout's been approved

363
00:13:23,000 --> 00:13:25,000
for his next rank

364
00:13:25,000 --> 00:13:27,000
or a merit badge has been filed,

365
00:13:27,000 --> 00:13:29,000
as soon as that's all been reported

366
00:13:29,000 --> 00:13:31,000
and you have the badges in hand,

367
00:13:31,000 --> 00:13:33,000
get them to the scouts.

368
00:13:33,000 --> 00:13:35,000
For a more formal Quartz of Honor,

369
00:13:35,000 --> 00:13:37,000
I suggest an hour is maximum

370
00:13:37,000 --> 00:13:39,000
and less is even better.

371
00:13:39,000 --> 00:13:41,000
that the program needs to be appropriately

372
00:13:41,000 --> 00:13:43,000
formal without being way

373
00:13:43,000 --> 00:13:45,000
dramatically overblown.

374
00:13:45,000 --> 00:13:47,000
that the scouts need to run their own Quartz of Honor.

375
00:13:47,000 --> 00:13:49,000
that if we're going to

376
00:13:49,000 --> 00:13:51,000
combine the Quartz of Honor with a business meeting

377
00:13:51,000 --> 00:13:53,000
for parents or something like that,

378
00:13:53,000 --> 00:13:55,000
we need to keep that part of the program

379
00:13:55,000 --> 00:13:57,000
brief and meaningful.

380
00:13:57,000 --> 00:13:59,000
Busy families with lots on the schedule

381
00:13:59,000 --> 00:14:01,000
usually appreciated if you

382
00:14:01,000 --> 00:14:03,000
have a Quartz of Honor on your standard

383
00:14:03,000 --> 00:14:05,000
meeting night because that's already

384
00:14:05,000 --> 00:14:07,000
on their calendar. Holding regular

385
00:14:07,000 --> 00:14:09,000
Quartz of Honor every quarter

386
00:14:09,000 --> 00:14:11,000
is a good practice

387
00:14:11,000 --> 00:14:13,000
and it keeps everybody in touch with what's going on

388
00:14:13,000 --> 00:14:15,000
and it gives you a chance to recognize

389
00:14:15,000 --> 00:14:17,000
scouts in a timely manner

390
00:14:17,000 --> 00:14:19,000
at that formal Quartz of Honor.

391
00:14:19,000 --> 00:14:21,000
If the attendance at your Quartz of Honor

392
00:14:21,000 --> 00:14:23,000
is a little thin, it may not be

393
00:14:23,000 --> 00:14:25,000
the fault of the audience. it may be

394
00:14:25,000 --> 00:14:27,000
adjustments or fixes that you need

395
00:14:27,000 --> 00:14:29,000
to make to make it worthwhile

396
00:14:29,000 --> 00:14:31,000
and meaningful to attend.

397
00:14:59,000 --> 00:15:01,000
That's right. it's time

398
00:15:01,000 --> 00:15:03,000
for a Scoutmasters Minute.

399
00:15:23,000 --> 00:15:25,000
As I said earlier, my new book, The Scouting Journey,

400
00:15:25,000 --> 00:15:27,000
is going to be published towards the middle of next month.

401
00:15:27,000 --> 00:15:29,000
What it is is it's my attempt

402
00:15:29,000 --> 00:15:31,000
to take about 1600

403
00:15:31,000 --> 00:15:33,000
blog posts

404
00:15:33,000 --> 00:15:35,000
and 191 podcasts

405
00:15:35,000 --> 00:15:37,000
and

406
00:15:37,000 --> 00:15:39,000
and boil it all down into one good, clear,

407
00:15:39,000 --> 00:15:41,000
distinct vision. that is something

408
00:15:41,000 --> 00:15:43,000
that a Scouter could pick up

409
00:15:43,000 --> 00:15:45,000
and hopefully understand something of the width

410
00:15:45,000 --> 00:15:47,000
and the breadth of what Scouting

411
00:15:47,000 --> 00:15:49,000
is supposed to do for our young people,

412
00:15:49,000 --> 00:15:51,000
and to give them solid ideas about

413
00:15:51,000 --> 00:15:53,000
helping them inform

414
00:15:53,000 --> 00:15:55,000
the work that they do as Scouters.

415
00:15:55,000 --> 00:15:57,000
Let me share with you

416
00:15:57,000 --> 00:15:59,000
something out of the book that I think is important

417
00:15:59,000 --> 00:16:01,000
for all Scouters to understand, and that is

418
00:16:01,000 --> 00:16:03,000
the Scouter as a Guide.

419
00:16:03,000 --> 00:16:05,000
We're talking about giving them a general push

420
00:16:05,000 --> 00:16:07,000
in the right direction, a suggestion

421
00:16:07,000 --> 00:16:09,000
rather than a command,

422
00:16:09,000 --> 00:16:11,000
a question asked rather than an answer

423
00:16:11,000 --> 00:16:13,000
given, and then letting them

424
00:16:13,000 --> 00:16:15,000
find their own way,

425
00:16:15,000 --> 00:16:17,000
and I think that's what sets Scouting apart

426
00:16:17,000 --> 00:16:19,000
from so many other things that children do.

427
00:16:19,000 --> 00:16:21,000
so imagine

428
00:16:21,000 --> 00:16:23,000
you're on a bus tour of some

429
00:16:23,000 --> 00:16:25,000
city- okay- and you're seated

430
00:16:25,000 --> 00:16:27,000
in an air-conditioned

431
00:16:27,000 --> 00:16:29,000
motor coach, listening to a guide

432
00:16:29,000 --> 00:16:31,000
drone on and on, the way the guide

433
00:16:31,000 --> 00:16:33,000
sometimes do, and the bus

434
00:16:33,000 --> 00:16:35,000
is rolling along and the guide is explaining

435
00:16:35,000 --> 00:16:37,000
each landmark that we pass

436
00:16:37,000 --> 00:16:39,000
in detail, so we're not going to miss

437
00:16:39,000 --> 00:16:41,000
anything. in fact,

438
00:16:41,000 --> 00:16:43,000
there's so much explaining

439
00:16:43,000 --> 00:16:45,000
that there's not a whole lot of time left

440
00:16:45,000 --> 00:16:47,000
for us to ask questions. and the guide

441
00:16:47,000 --> 00:16:49,000
is a great guide. they stick to the script.

442
00:16:49,000 --> 00:16:51,000
and we sit behind the

443
00:16:51,000 --> 00:16:53,000
tinted windows of our bus, dutifully turning

444
00:16:53,000 --> 00:16:55,000
our heads to the left and looking over

445
00:16:55,000 --> 00:16:57,000
at that building over there

446
00:16:57,000 --> 00:16:59,000
and to the right, and we see that building over there

447
00:16:59,000 --> 00:17:01,000
and after a while the tour is over.

448
00:17:01,000 --> 00:17:03,000
now contrast that bus tour

449
00:17:03,000 --> 00:17:05,000
with a hike that's

450
00:17:05,000 --> 00:17:07,000
guided by some knowledgeable

451
00:17:07,000 --> 00:17:09,000
outdoorsman. our group takes the lead

452
00:17:09,000 --> 00:17:11,000
and finds the trail. now where the trail

453
00:17:11,000 --> 00:17:13,000
branches, our guide will let

454
00:17:13,000 --> 00:17:15,000
us know which way to go

455
00:17:15,000 --> 00:17:17,000
if we can't figure it out on our own,

456
00:17:17,000 --> 00:17:19,000
that he doesn't mind if we stop now

457
00:17:19,000 --> 00:17:21,000
and then to admire a flower or take

458
00:17:21,000 --> 00:17:23,000
in the view, and he'll happily tell us

459
00:17:23,000 --> 00:17:25,000
about anything we're looking at if we ask.

460
00:17:25,000 --> 00:17:27,000
our outdoorsman is not going to

461
00:17:27,000 --> 00:17:29,000
volunteer a whole lot of information.

462
00:17:29,000 --> 00:17:31,000
he'll drop a hint here and there.

463
00:17:31,000 --> 00:17:33,000
he'll ask questions.

464
00:17:33,000 --> 00:17:35,000
we may miss a couple of things

465
00:17:35,000 --> 00:17:37,000
along the way and we might pass by

466
00:17:37,000 --> 00:17:39,000
interesting things, but we'll probably get more

467
00:17:39,000 --> 00:17:41,000
out of what we discovered

468
00:17:41,000 --> 00:17:43,000
on the hike than the things that the

469
00:17:43,000 --> 00:17:45,000
tour guide told us on the bus.

470
00:17:45,000 --> 00:17:47,000
so, like that

471
00:17:47,000 --> 00:17:49,000
outdoorsman, like the guide,

472
00:17:49,000 --> 00:17:51,000
scouters are more responsive

473
00:17:51,000 --> 00:17:53,000
than directive.

474
00:17:53,000 --> 00:17:55,000
so it's that spirit of the guide

475
00:17:55,000 --> 00:17:57,000
that I think is important for us

476
00:17:57,000 --> 00:17:59,000
to think about as scouters.

477
00:17:59,000 --> 00:18:01,000
every individual scout is different

478
00:18:01,000 --> 00:18:03,000
and every group of scouts has different needs.

479
00:18:03,000 --> 00:18:05,000
now, when we're working with younger scouts,

480
00:18:05,000 --> 00:18:07,000
our style is still going to be the guide.

481
00:18:07,000 --> 00:18:09,000
it's going to be a little more directive

482
00:18:09,000 --> 00:18:11,000
and as they grow older,

483
00:18:11,000 --> 00:18:13,000
hopefully we've laid the basis

484
00:18:13,000 --> 00:18:15,000
for them to guide themselves

485
00:18:15,000 --> 00:18:17,000
and to find their own way

486
00:18:17,000 --> 00:18:19,000
and to discover what scouting is all about.

487
00:18:19,000 --> 00:18:21,000
and pretty soon we've kind of

488
00:18:21,000 --> 00:18:23,000
faded into the background

489
00:18:23,000 --> 00:18:25,000
and our role is the same as

490
00:18:25,000 --> 00:18:27,000
that outdoorsman, that guide

491
00:18:27,000 --> 00:18:29,000
who lets the group take the lead

492
00:18:29,000 --> 00:18:31,000
and find the trail and discover these things

493
00:18:31,000 --> 00:18:33,000
for themselves-

494
00:18:45,000 --> 00:19:05,000
music,

